fs/fat: reset the open-file cursor after shrinking

fat_dirshrink() changes the cluster chain without updating the fat_file_s
cursor cached by fat_get_sectors().  If the file was last accessed in the
removed suffix, a later write at or beyond that logical cluster offset starts
from the now-free cached cluster instead of traversing from ff_startcluster.
The write then either returns EIO or attaches storage to the wrong chain.

SQLite VACUUM reliably exposes this: ftruncate() shrinks the database, then
later transactions grow it through the stale cursor.  Depending on the old
cursor position, the first failure is delayed by several transactions.  A
damaged chain can also leave a later truncate or sync walking indefinitely.

Flush and invalidate the per-file sector buffer before changing the chain so
dirty data cannot be written into a cluster after it has been freed.  Once the
shrink succeeds, reset the traversal cursor to the start of the retained
chain.  A zero-length truncate also has to clear ff_startcluster because
fat_dirtruncate() clears only the on-media directory entry.  Keep filep->f_pos
unchanged as required by ftruncate(); fat_get_sectors() will traverse or grow
from the rebuilt cursor when I/O resumes.

--- a/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c
+++ b/fs/fat/fs_fat32.c
@@ -1896,6 +1896,17 @@ static int fat_truncate(FAR struct file *filep, off_t length)
       FAR uint8_t *direntry;
       int ndx;
 
+      /* The sector buffer and cluster cursor may still describe the suffix
+       * which is about to be freed.  Publish dirty data while the old chain
+       * is valid, then discard that cached location before changing it.
+       */
+
+      ret = fat_ffcacheinvalidate(fs, ff);
+      if (ret < 0)
+        {
+          goto errout_with_lock;
+        }
+
       /* We are shrinking the file.
        *
        * Read the directory entry into the fs_buffer.
@@ -1933,10 +1944,21 @@ static int fat_truncate(FAR struct file *filep, off_t length)
       if (ret >= 0)
         {
           /* The truncation has completed without error.  Update the file
-           * size.
+           * size and rebuild the open-file traversal cursor.  The logical
+           * file position is intentionally unchanged by ftruncate().
            */
 
+          if (length == 0)
+            {
+              ff->ff_startcluster = 0;
+            }
+
+          ff->ff_currentcluster   = ff->ff_startcluster;
+          ff->ff_currentsector    = 0;
+          ff->ff_sectorsincluster = fs->fs_fatsecperclus;
+          ff->ff_cachesector      = 0;
+          ff->ff_pos              = 0;
           ff->ff_size = length;
           ret = OK;
         }
     }
